<<~ ahu #/law>>

! Tiddler Carriage ~ the record syncs, a peer fetches the bytes

''Register: Model-Standing.'' A tasked spirit carried one sentence to the mesh and measured whether it holds. Every claim below names a file and a line, a socket answer, or a directory listing; a claim without one names itself as inference.

''The operator's words, verbatim (quote license):''

> "All 'tiddler carries' flow through CRDTs into the magical federated mesh (private operator/user or public data, 'it's all tiddlers' unless its CID/CAD NOT in CRDT)."

''THE LAW (RFC-2119).''

# ''A tiddler stands as a CRDT record.'' Every carry the house makes — a meme, a `.md`, a `.tid`, a POINTER to a binary — lands as a record in a bag doc (`LarTiddlerRecord`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/tiddler-store.ts`) and MUST sync as that doc syncs. Nothing rides beside the CRDT as a second carriage of record.
# ''A blob holds what a CID names.'' The bytes behind a pointer rest in a content-addressed tier — cleartext `cid/` (`sha256(bytes)` hex, `packages/lararium-node/src/node-cas.ts`) or ciphertext `@cad` (`blake3:` over the ciphertext, `packages/lararium-node/src/ciphertext-cas-seal.ts`) — and MUST NOT ride the CRDT. The pointer carries `textCid` · `_integrity` · `size` · `type` · `_is_skinny` and no `text` (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/blob-carriage#/proposed-law">> clause 3).
# ''The RECORD syncs by the share policy.'' A fleet peer (same-operator, a signed device edge) receives every plane; a cross-operator peer receives the deterministically-federatable public shelf, and a contracted MEMBER blind-transits a provably-sealed private plane (`packages/lararium-node/src/self-slot-share.ts`; the policy closure at `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts:434-462`). The pointer crosses WITH the record, riding as a field of the record.
# ''A peer FETCHES the BYTES on request; nothing syncs them with the record.'' A peer that holds the pointer and the read cap MUST hold a door to fetch the blob by cid, and MUST verify `hash(bytes) == cid` before it trusts a byte (the bulb's own law, `packages/lararium-node/src/bulb.ts:15-18`, `assembleBulb`; the lazy resolver's, `packages/lararium-tw5/src/lazy-resolver.ts`). A fetch MAY fail (a miss reads PENDING and a later render re-tries); a fetch MUST NOT hand back unverified bytes.
# ''By which cap.'' The cid carries the verify-cap to anyone (a content address self-proves). A PUBLIC blob's bytes ride the public floor under `pull ≠ read` — "even public keeps the gate" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution#/cad-storage">>). A PRIVATE blob's bytes ride as ciphertext: the carry-cap (a proven MEMBER over a provably-sealed plane, `carrierShareDecision`) moves it; the read-cap (the message key on the keyhive lane) opens it; the two never fuse (#/private-plane).
# ''Retention.'' A blob stays RETAINED while any record in a locally-held bag references its cid, or a PIN cap with a lifetime names it, or the genesis manifest names it; an unreferenced, unpinned blob MAY sweep after a grace; `DROP <bag>` releases that bag's references by taking its records out of the count (#/pin-and-release).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/measured>>

!! Measured ~ three steps, the lines the sockets and dirs answered

''Step 1 — THE FLEET CROSSING'' (`tests/e2e/blob-follows-pointer.test.ts`, run 2026-09-11 over `tests/harness/instance.ts` `openStaged`, the admit rite copied from `meme-two-vessel-bag`):

* A LOADs `photo.png` + `photo.png.meta` (`type: image/png`, 70 bytes) through the one door — `act LOAD --source-uri …/bags/lares/t.witness.blob --to lar:///ha.ka.ba/bags/lares`. The receipt: `{"ok":true,…,"count":1,"titles":["lar:///t.witness.blob/photo"]}`.
* A's `cid/` (`<rootA>/data/lares/vessel/cas`): `5 blobs; raw-cid true · base64-cid false` — the CID keys the RAW bytes (`497790947d4666…`), the sibling's blob law standing in the stager.
* A projects `bags/lares/t.witness.blob/photo.png · photo.png.meta`; the `.meta` reads `{"_integrity":"ni:///sha-256;SXeQlH1GZnYM4488AOhSxx_bZsroSbro6e3jUnGeFYE","_is_skinny":"yes","_source_ext":".png","size":"70","textCid":"497790947d4666760ce38f3c00e852c71fdb66cae849bae8e9ede352719e1581","title":"lar:///t.witness.blob/photo","type":"image/png"}` — no `text`, no `_canonical_uri`.
* B, after sync: `wiki which lar:///t.witness.blob/photo` → `primary: lar:///ha.ka.ba/bags/lares`. ''The RECORD crossed.'' B projects `bags/lares/t.witness.blob/photo.png.meta` alone with the SAME fields — the projector's PENDING shape (the `.meta`, no content file).
* B's `cid/`: `blobs on B: 4 (genesis + staged); holds 497790947d466676…: false`. `photo.png beside the .meta on B's disk: false`. B's boot log carries no line naming a cas fetch. `lares bag cas --all --json` on B: `"blobs":4,"referenced":0,"unreferenced":4,…` with all four `"protected":true` (the genesis engine + plugins). ''The BYTES did not cross, by any door.''
* Verdict: `Tests 5 passed | 1 expected fail (6)` — the vector "the BYTES follow the pointer: B's cid/ holds the blob a fetch door carried" holds as `test.fails`; the day a door lands it reads "expected to fail" and retires.
* A side-measure, reported for the two-vessel seat and NOT ruled here: a pointer LOADed into `bags/lares` on A BEFORE B dials reads `wiki which → lar:///ha.ka.ba/bags/lares` at once, and `(not found)` on A itself once B has stood (three runs); the same LOAD issued after B stands lands and crosses.

''Step 2 — THE CROSS-OPERATOR CROSSING.'' The bulb stands as the one content-addressed public read-face the house serves (`GET /bulb/<cid>.bin`, `packages/lararium-node/src/bulb-read-face.ts`), and it answers off `blobByCid` built from `buildBulb(bulb)` — the genesis manifest's blobs alone (`bulb-read-face.ts:52-55`); a hearth's `cid/` never reaches a Herm.

* `packages/lararium-node/tests/bulb-serves-boot-cas-alone.test.ts` (green first run): a genesis cid → `200` with bytes that re-hash to the cid (CONTROL); a cid written into the SAME vessel's `cid/` dir but absent from the manifest → `404 unknown or stale bulb cid`.
* `tools/mesh-scenarios.sh run_meme` gained one step, gated behind the bag seam it already reports. Run 2026-09-11 (`bash tools/mesh-scenarios.sh meme`, exit 0, every container down): A LOADs the same `.png` inside the mesh; `A staged: blobs 5 · referenced 1 · unreferenced 4 · pending 0 · bytes 3308699 · protected (genesis) 4`; `herm-source GET /bulb/497790947d466676….bin → 404` — GAP "the bulb serves the boot CAS alone — A's blob draws 404; no public-floor cid door stands". The fetch step behind it reads GAP "unwalkable until the crossing stands — B holds no pointer to fetch for". The scenario's own finding stands unchanged: two contracted operators hold two `lares` docs (`automerge:FVGJY9…` on A, `automerge:enPoFp…` on B) and the relation carries no bag between them.

''Step 3 — PIN AND RELEASE'' — built red-first; #/pin-and-release carries the reds and the code.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-seam>>

!! The seam ~ where a blob fetch attaches

No door carries a cleartext `sha256` blob across the fleet today. The three places a fetch would attach, each read from source:

# ''The resolver's fallback chain'' — `packages/lararium-node/src/sovereign-island-model.ts:91-95`: the worker's `resolveByCid` reads the runtime `cas` dir, then the corpus CAS dir, and returns `null`. Its own comment names the composition: "wrap this local read with `makeCidResolver(localRead, casTransitTransport, cacheWriteThrough)` (@lararium/mesh)" — `packages/lararium-mesh/src/cas-transit.ts:104-118` already composes local-first → transit → verify → write-through. The worker holds no transport; the daemon does. The seam sits at the daemon tier where `makeCidResolver` gets a transport.
# ''The fleet dial's channel'' — `packages/lararium-node/src/nexus-client-dial.ts`: Socket A carries Automerge sync alone (`LarWSClientAdapter` onto `repo.networkSubsystem`); it carries no request/response frame a `cas-want-block` could ride. Socket B (`packages/lararium-node/src/cas-wire.ts`, `CAS_WANT_BLOCK` → `CAS_BLOCK` | `CAS_MU`) carries a blob peer-to-peer over the `MembershipChannel` — but gates on `docIdForCiphertextCid(cid)` through the seal registry and serves off the `cad/` dir (`cas-wire.ts` `decideAndServeWantBlock`), so a cleartext `sha256` cid draws Mu (`readCasBlobFromFs(cid, deps.cadDir)` → null → the void). The honest fleet door: the SAME five messages with the gate reading the fleet class (`same-operator` → serve off `cid/`; cross-operator → the sealed lane as today) and the fetcher verifying `sha256(bytes) == cid` (`verifyCiphertextCid` reads `blake3:` only — a `sha256` branch, or `parseDigest` widened).
# ''The bulb's read-face'' — `bulb-read-face.ts:118-124` matches `^/bulb/([0-9a-f]{64})\.bin$` against the boot manifest. A public-floor `GET /cas/<cid>.bin` served off the holder's `cid/` tier (verify-on-pull, `cache-control: immutable`) opens the cross-operator door for a PUBLIC pointer — and the peer's resolver reaches it through the SAME `makeCidResolver` transport (`discover` = the crossroads pointer's holder hint; `fetchBlock` = the GET).

''Not built, and why.'' The fetch that fits the files this spirit owns (`node-cas.ts` + the dial) would still need the worker resolver (`sovereign-island-model.ts`) or the vessel boot (`open-node-vessel.ts`) to hand the transport in — files a sibling holds in flight. A door built on one side of that fence measures nothing. Held.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/pin-and-release>>

!! Pin and release ~ the derived count, the sweep, the PIN cap held

''The reference count DERIVES; no field stores it.'' `casReferences(entries)` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/cas.ts`) walks every locally-held record (`CompositeStore.entries()` — title · bagId · record) and reads `casCidOfRecord`: `textCid` first, else a `lar:///…/cid/<hash>` `_canonical_uri` — the SAME discrimination the lazy resolver's `skinnyCid` makes — yielding `Map<cid, Set<"<bagId> <title>">>`. A record held by two bags counts twice, so `DROP` of one leaves the other. `summarizeCas(blobs, refs)` folds a store listing into `blobs · referenced · unreferenced · pending · bytes` — ''pending'' names a referenced cid the store LACKS, the fleet peer's shape after a record crossed (B above reads `pending 1` once its projection lands).

''The sweep.'' `casSweep({ casDir, references, protect, graceMs, dryRun })` (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-cas.ts`) marks from the derived references + the genesis manifest's cids (`readGenesisManifest(genesisDir).blobs`, the engine + plugins the bulb ships) and sweeps only a `[0-9a-f]{64}`-named file with zero references, outside the protect set, older than the grace. `listCasBlobs(casDir)` lists cid + size. Nothing in the daemon CALLS the sweep yet — the residency verbs hold its seat (`DROP` releases; a sweep tick rides the Ea breath), held.

''The reds, quoted.'' `packages/lararium-mesh/tests/cas-references.test.ts` — `TypeError: casReferences is not a function` (4/4) → 4 green. `packages/lararium-node/tests/node-cas-sweep.test.ts` — `TypeError: casSweep is not a function` (5/5) → 5 green: land a pointer → the blob KEPT; DROP the bag → `swept: [cid]`, the file gone; CONTROL a genesis blob → `protected: [cid]`, never swept; CONTROL a blob two bags reference → survives DROP of one; the grace holds a young orphan; a dry run names and deletes nothing; a non-cid name in the dir is never touched. `packages/lares-cli/tests/cas-read.test.ts` — `Cannot find module '/src/commands/cas.js'` → green.

''The read.'' `lares bag cas [--all]` (`packages/lares-cli/src/commands/cas.ts`, seated in `bag.ts`'s table) — pure local inspection, no daemon: it lists `larCasDir()` and derives references from the DISK PROJECTION (`.tid` / `.meta` under `bags/` + `wikis/`), names the genesis cids protected, and prints `blobs N · referenced N · unreferenced N · pending N · bytes N · protected (genesis) N`. The projection reads as-of-its-last-write and sites only loci-pathed records, so this read NEVER sweeps; the authoritative count comes off `casReferences(composite.entries())` where the composite stands.

''The PIN cap — shape held, not built.'' A PIN reads as a cap with a lifetime (the cap-tier keystone, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier#/the-keystone">>): `{ cid, tier, holder, expiry }` — a tag bound to the tier of the bag whose pointer it pins (map-tier = body-tier, a declared tier only tightens), carried on the daemon board as a per-writer max-register like a lease epoch, read by the sweep as a third mark beside the derived references and the genesis set. A pack PIN names the descriptor's cid and the sweep follows its member list. Its lifetime follows the grace the operator sets (#/questions 1).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/private-plane>>

!! The private plane ~ what `@cad` ciphertext means for a fleet peer's fetch

''Verify-cap ⊥ read-cap.'' `installSealedBody` (`packages/lararium-node/src/ciphertext-cas-seal.ts:66-78`) message-locks the plaintext to the per-Nexus epoch secret, content-addresses the CIPHERTEXT (`cid = blake3:<hex>`), writes it to the `cad/` tier, and registers `docIdForCiphertextCid(cid)` into the live `SealedPlaneRegistry` AS A SIDE-EFFECT — the only door into the sealed set, so a cleartext body never registers. It returns the `readCap` (32 bytes) to the caller alone; the relay serves + verifies secret-free (`verifyCiphertextCid`, `cas-wire.ts`). FAIL-CLOSED with no keyring (`keyring.current()` throws; the body stays cleartext-local — the #51 Fix=B ruling: cas-stage = CLEARTEXT LOCAL vs `@cad` seal = CIPHERTEXT FEDERATION).

''What a fleet peer fetches.'' A same-operator peer full-syncs the record; for the BYTES it holds the read-cap by construction (the keyring rides the fleet) and would carry the ciphertext over Socket B's `cas-want-block` — the ONE blob wire that stands — then decrypt locally. A cross-operator MEMBER carries the same ciphertext and reads nothing (carry ⊥ read); a STRANGER draws the byte-identical Mu (denial ≡ satiety, `cas-wire.ts`). ''Measured:'' `packages/lararium-node/tests/cas-wire.test.ts` + `ciphertext-cas-seal.test.ts` stand green in the node suite (150 files); `cas-transit.test.ts` in mesh. ''Named, not measured:'' no live vessel wires `makeCidResolver` over the carriage channel — the sealed fetch and the cleartext fetch attach at the SAME seam (#/the-seam 1).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/questions>>

!! Questions ~ only the operator answers

# ''The grace period.'' `casSweep` takes `graceMs` and nothing sets it. The grace protects a staged body whose verb has not landed yet; a PIN cap's lifetime rides the same number. One value for the house, or per tier?
# ''Prefetch or fetch-on-read.'' When a pointer's record crosses to a fleet peer, does the peer PREFETCH the bytes for every pointer it receives (the mirror shape — B's disk then carries `photo.png` beside the `.meta`, and `bag cas` reads `pending 0`), or fetch on the first `getTiddlerText` render (the lazy shape — B's disk carries the `.meta` alone until a read)? The projector already writes the PENDING shape for the second; the first needs a sync-time hook.
# ''What the bulb serves.'' Today the boot CAS alone (measured). Does the public read-face grow a `GET /cas/<cid>.bin` off the holder's `cid/` for crossroads pointers — every public blob a hearth stages served by that hearth, verify-on-pull — or does a public blob first travel to a Herm (a re-share leg, the `cas-have` announce) so a hearth never serves its own floor?

<<~/ahu>>

